// === NAME - DESCRIPTION:  ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
// Randomize probabilities
$pA = round(rand(0.2, 0.7), 2);
$pB = round(rand(0.2, 0.7), 2);

// Ensure P(A and B) is valid: must be <= min(pA, pB)
$maxOverlap = min($pA, $pB);
$pAnd = round(rand(0.05, $maxOverlap), 2);

// Calculate P(A or B)
$pOr = $pA + $pB - $pAnd;

// For the conceptual part
$questions[1][0] = "It counts the outcomes in the intersection twice.";
$questions[1][1] = "It fails to account for the fact that A and B are mutually exclusive.";
$questions[1][2] = "It only counts the outcomes that are in A but not in B.";
$questions[1][3] = "It subtracts the intersection instead of adding it.";

$answer[0] = $pOr;
$answer[1] = 0;

$anstypes = "number,choices";

// Formatting for display
$pA_disp = sprintf("%.2f", $pA);
$pB_disp = sprintf("%.2f", $pB);
$pAnd_disp = sprintf("%.2f", $pAnd);

// Solution guide
$solutionguide = "To find the probability of the union of two events A and B, we use the Addition Rule: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B). Substituting the given values: P(A or B) = $pA_disp + $pB_disp - $pAnd_disp = " . sprintf("%.2f", $pOr) . ". We subtract the intersection because those outcomes are included in both P(A) and P(B); adding the two probabilities without subtracting the overlap counts those outcomes twice.";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
In a probability experiment, two events A and B are defined. The probabilities of these events and their intersection are given as:
<ul>
  <li>`P(A) =` `$pA_disp`</li>
  <li>`P(B) =` `$pB_disp`</li>
  <li>`P(A \cap B) =` `$pAnd_disp`</li>
</ul>

(a) Calculate the probability that event A or event B occurs:
`P(A \cup B) =` $answerbox[0]

<p>
(b) If a student simply added `P(A) + P(B)` to find the answer to part (a), why would this result be incorrect?
$answerbox[1]
</p>

