</table>

<p>(a) Based on the tables, what was the total number of participants in the study?</p>
Total participants: $answerbox[0]

<p>(b) What is the exact percentage of participants who chose $targetCat? (Round to one decimal place if necessary.)</p>
Percentage for $targetCat: $answerbox[1]%

<p>(c) If you were to draw a bar graph of the counts and a bar graph of the percentages, the overall shape of the two graphs would be identical. Why is this the case?</p>
$answerbox[2]

// === ANSWER ===
$countsList = arraytolist($counts, ' + ');
$solutionguide = 'Part (a): To find the total group size, sum the counts from Table 1: ' . $countsList . ' = ' . $total . '. Alternatively, if you know one category\'s count and its percentage, you can divide the count by the decimal percentage.
Part (b): The percentage for ' . $targetCat . ' is calculated as (' . $targetCount . ' / ' . $total . ') * 100 = ' . round($targetPct, 1) . '%.
Part (c): Because every count is divided by the same constant (the total sample size) to get the percentage, the relative heights of the bars do not change, preserving the shape of the distribution.';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Normal Distribution - Critical Z and invNorm Area Logic ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
loadlibrary("stats");

// Part 1: Right-tail area to left-tail area conversion
$rightArea = 0.05 + (rand(0, 40) / 1000); // 0.050 to 0.090
$leftArea = 1 - $rightArea;

// Part 2: Middle percentage to tail areas
$midPct = randfrom([0.90, 0.95, 0.98, 0.99]);
$tailArea = (1 - $midPct) / 2;
$zMid = abs(invnormalcdf($tailArea, 0, 1));

$anstypes = "number,number,number";

// Part 1 answers
$answer[0] = round($leftArea, 4);
$answer[1] = round(invnormalcdf($leftArea, 0, 1), 3);

// Part 2 answer
$answer[2] = round($zMid, 3);

// Formatting for display
$rightAreaDisp = sprintf("%.4f", $rightArea);
$midPctDisp = ($midPct * 100) . "%";

// Solution Guide
$solutionguide = "Part (a): The invNorm function calculates the area to the left of a z-score. To find the z-score for a right-tail area of $rightAreaDisp, we must use the complement: 1 - $rightAreaDisp = " . sprintf("%.4f", $leftArea) . ". Then, z = invNorm(" . sprintf("%.4f", $leftArea) . ", 0, 1).
Part (b): For a middle area of $midPctDisp, the remaining area in the two tails combined is " . sprintf("%.4f", 1 - $midPct) . ". Each single tail contains half of that: " . sprintf("%.4f", $tailArea) . ". The critical z-score is the value that bounds this tail area, found via invNorm(" . sprintf("%.4f", $tailArea) . ", 0, 1), which gives " . round($zMid, 3) . " (taking the absolute value for the positive bound).";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Consider the standard normal distribution (mean `\mu = 0` and standard deviation `\sigma = 1`).</p>

<p><b>Part (a):</b> You want to find the critical z-score such that the area to the <i>right</i> of z is `$rightAreaDisp`.</p>
<p>Since the invNorm function calculates the area to the <i>left</i> of a z-score, what area should you enter into the function? <br>
Area = $answerbox[0]</p>
<p>Using that area, find the critical z-score (round to 3 decimal places): <br>
z = $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>Part (b):</b> Find the positive z-score that bounds the middle `$midPctDisp` of the standard normal curve (round to 3 decimal places): <br>
z = $answerbox[2]</p>

